<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Türkmenbaşy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krasnovodsk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Türkmenbaşy, known until 1993 as Krasnovodsk, is a port city on the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. Founded as a Russian fort in 1869, it became the western terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-caspian-railway">Trans-Caspian Railway</a> and the main link across the Caspian to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. In 1918 it was held by the anti-Bolshevik Transcaspian government, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/26-baku-commissars">26 Baku Commissars</a> were shot nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turkmenbasy.jpg" alt="Türkmenbaşy" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bohndorf Film-Team · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hafenviertel_Türkmenbaschy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Türkmenbaşy lies on the Türkmenbaşy Gulf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> at an elevation of 27 metres, on the site where the dry bed of a former mouth of the Amu-Darya River once emptied into the sea. It has a cold desert climate with hot summers and somewhat chilly winters, and average annual precipitation of 125 millimetres. The city has been known by several names: Shagadam, Kyzyl-Su, meaning Red Water, Krasnovodsk, its Russian translation, and Türkmenbaşy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1717 the Russian prince Alexander Bekovich-Cherkassky landed here and set up a secret fortified settlement. He intended to march an army up the dry riverbed and conquer the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>. The expedition failed, and the Russians abandoned the settlement for over 150 years.</p><p>In 1869 the Russians came a second time, captured the settlement and named their fort Krasnovodsk. It served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> as a base of operations against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and against the semi-nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-caspian-railway">Trans-Caspian Railway</a> had begun at Uzun-Ada on the Caspian, and its terminus was shifted north to the harbour at Krasnovodsk. The Russian station building dates from 1895, and the Church of St. Michael the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> was moved to the town from Uzun-Ada in the same year. The Armenian Apostolic church was built in 1903.</p><p>The town fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in February 1920. Krasnovodsk Oblast was formed on 21 November 1939 and was repeatedly abolished and restored until 1988. In 1942 a large part of the Polish Anders' Army, together with many civilians, was evacuated from the USSR through the port to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. On 8 October 1993 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saparmurat-niyazov">Saparmurat Niyazov</a> renamed the city Türkmenbaşy, meaning Head of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a>, after his own self-proclaimed title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Türkmenbaşy is a major port in Balkan Province in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. Its seaport is Turkmenistan's main sea link to the west, with ferries across the Caspian to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, about 260 kilometres away, and it is the western terminus of the Trans-Caspian railway, which runs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashgabat">Ashgabat</a>. The country's largest oil refinery is here. Most of the population are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a>, who are Sunni Muslim, with Russian, Armenian and Azerbaijani minorities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Türkmenbaşy, Turkmenistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its leaders fled, were captured and shot near <b>Krasnovodsk</b> in September 1918, becoming the 26 Baku Commissars of Soviet legend.</blockquote><a href="/state/baku-commune">Baku Commune</a></li><li><blockquote>The government controlled the Trans-Caspian railway from <b>Krasnovodsk</b> towards Merv, relying on Turkmen and Russian volunteers and a small British force under General Wilfrid Malleson.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaspian-government">Transcaspian Government</a></li></ul></section>
